{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NwmYkkHWH7qFMCCAkv5Sjx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — causal_annot_error_attrib\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract: 2026-07-21, paper: 2026-07-28)\n5\t- Secondary: ARR October 2026 (~2026-10-15, commit to NAACL 2027 / ACL 2027)\n6\t- Tertiary: ICWSM 2027 Round 2 (2026-09-15)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tMulti-Class Annotation Errors Are Not Created Equal: Bias Taxonomy, Sensitivity Analysis, and Correction Guidance for LLM-Annotated Causal Studies\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n12\t\n13\t1. **Multi-class bias taxonomy**: For K≥3 annotation variables, the direction and magnitude of downstream bias depend on the interaction between the K×K confusion matrix structure and the DAG topology — breaking guarantees that hold in the binary case. We provide closed-form bias expressions for K=3 and matrix-form expressions with interpretability tools for general K, across 6 canonical social-science DAG topologies (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, instrument, front-door).\n14\t\n15\t2. **Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs)**: A sensitivity analysis framework for annotation-induced bias. ASVs quantify the minimum degree of differential annotation error needed to overturn a downstream conclusion, parameterized by a reference confusion matrix and amplification factor δ. Reported alongside estimates, analogous to E-values for unmeasured confounding. Formal proofs (monotonicity + sharpness) for 3 priority topologies; MC-validated conjectures for the remaining 3.\n16\t\n17\t3. **Correction guidance framework**: A principled decision framework mapping {DAG topology × error structure × ASV} to the appropriate correction method (DSL, PPI, MLA, naive plug-in), characterizing regimes where each applies and where no existing method suffices — including dependence-aware multi-LLM correction as an open problem.\n18\t\n19\t## Story Spine\n20\t\n21\t- **Problem**: LLMs are rapidly replacing human annotators in CSS, but Baumann et al. (2025) show 31% of downstream conclusions are already incorrect. Researchers lack diagnostic tools to answer: (a) which annotation error structures propagate bias under their causal design, and (b) how robust their conclusions are to annotation error. Kim et al. (ICML 2025) show cross-model errors are highly correlated (60% agreement when both err), meaning ensembles cannot escape systematic bias — researchers MUST analyze the error structure of their chosen annotator, which is exactly what our tools enable.\n22\t\n23\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Wood-Doughty et al. (2018) apply DAG reasoning to text classifier errors but produce no formal bias theorems (binary only). TeBlunthuis et al. (2024) provide the most complete binary treatment with MLA correction but are limited to K=2, single classifier. Lee & Wood-Doughty (2024) extend to categorical + LLM classifiers but cover only 1 DAG topology (unobserved confounding), with no systematic taxonomy or sensitivity analysis. VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds but only for binary risk ratios. Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) benchmark correction methods but treat error as monolithic — if different confusion matrix structures produce qualitatively different bias in different DAG topologies, unconditional benchmarking is itself misleading.\n24\t\n25\t- **Our framing**: Multi-class annotation errors are not created equal. The same confusion matrix that produces innocuous residual bias in one DAG topology can produce direction-reversing toxic bias in another. We provide the first systematic characterization of this interaction (the taxonomy), a tool to quantify robustness (ASVs), and guidance for when/how to correct (the framework). This is not a new correction method — it is the missing diagnostic layer that tells researchers which correction is appropriate, or whether correction is needed at all.\n26\t\n27\t- **Headline result template**: \"Across 6 DAG topologies with K=3,5,7, our bias expressions correctly predict bias direction and magnitude within 95% MC confidence intervals. On 3 CSS tasks annotated by 3 LLMs, ASVs provide calibrated robustness indicators, and our taxonomy identifies cases where commonly applied corrections would increase rather than decrease bias.\"\n28\t\n29\t## Core Insight (Lead Finding) — DUAL VERSION, gate on plan_001\n30\t\n31\t### Version A (≥1 topology shows direction reversal under K≥3 non-differential exposure misclassification)\n32\tThe binary guarantee that \"non-differential exposure misclassification biases toward the null\" (Bross 1954) breaks in the multi-class case. For specific confusion matrix structures in specific DAG topologies, K≥3 non-differential error can amplify or reverse effect estimates. Brenner (1993) identified this possibility for polytomous confounder misclassification (\"bias can go in either direction\"); we provide the first systematic characterization across 6 DAG topologies with exact conditions for each {C structure × topology} cell. Furthermore, binarizing a K-class variable to \"escape\" into the binary regime is itself hazardous — collapsing introduces new, direction-unpredictable bias (MVP evidence: Δ up to 0.103).\n33\t\n34\t### Version B (no direction reversal found across topologies)\n35\tMulti-class annotation error is structurally irreducible to the binary case: the downstream bias magnitude depends on the full K×K confusion matrix in ways that binary sensitivity/specificity cannot predict, and binarizing a K-class variable introduces new bias (MVP evidence: Δ up to 0.103). While non-differential error preserves bias direction across tested topologies (a useful practitioners' guarantee), the magnitude varies dramatically with confusion matrix structure (0.13–0.30 in confounding DAG alone), and this variation is topology-dependent. ASVs provide the missing robustness measure for this quantitative — not directional — uncertainty.\n36\t\n37\t**Gate**: plan_001 results determine which version. ≥2 topologies with reversal → A; 1 → A with that topology as lead; 0 → B. See D003.\n38\t\n39\t## MVP Evidence\n40\t\n41\t**Confounding DAG + K=3** (confounder misclassification):\n42\t- Theoretical bias expressions match MC perfectly (6/6 structures within 95% CI) — framework validated\n43\t- Binary collapse ≠ multi-class: Δ up to 0.103 (near-permutation C), proving K=3 retains more confounding control than binarization\n44\t- 100k random C search: confounding DAG bias is monotonically toward OVB — this topology is \"well-behaved\" (predictable direction)\n45\t- NOTE: Confounding DAG tests residual confounding, NOT bias-toward-null (Bross 1954). Criterion 2 (away-from-null bias) holds for binary too in this topology. See M001.\n46\t\n47\t**Exposure DAG K=3** (exposure misclassification — D002/D004 CONFIRMED):\n48\t- 100k random C: 85% violate bias-toward-null, 83.4% sign reversal, 14.1% amplification\n49\t- K=2 control: 6/6 toward null (binary guarantee holds)\n50\t- Worst case: β=1.0 → estimate=-0.84 (sign reversal + 2.83x amplification)\n51\t- Mechanism: near-permutation C causes label swap → effect direction flips (structurally impossible in K=2)\n52\t- **Two-DAG contrast**: confounding (monotonic, predictable) vs exposure (unpredictable, topology-dependent) — validates core claim\n53\t\n54\t## Venue Fit Risks\n55\t\n56\t- **AAAI**: Broad AI audience — emphasize general methodology for \"automated annotation measurement error,\" not LLM-specific. Frame as extending classical measurement error theory to modern automated labeling.\n57\t- **ARR/NAACL**: NLP audience — CSS track is natural fit. May want more empirical NLP experiments. Balance theory with practical LLM annotation scenarios.\n58\t- **Cross-discipline bridge**: Epidemiology (measurement error theory) × NLP (annotation errors). Epi reviewers check proof rigor; NLP reviewers check empirical relevance. Must serve both.\n59\t\n60\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n61\t\n62\t1. **\"Trivial extension of binary to multi-class\"** → Brenner (1993) proved direction reversal; our taxonomy provides the first systematic characterization across 6 topologies. Binarizing K-class introduces NEW unpredictable bias (MVP evidence). The complexity jump is structural: binary has 2 free parameters (sensitivity, specificity) → scalar bias; K=5 has 20 free parameters → matrix-valued bias requiring eigendecomposition for interpretation. [Experiments: plan_001, plan_008]\n63\t\n64\t2. **\"Lee & Wood-Doughty (2024) already did categorical\"** → They cover 1 topology (unobserved confounding) on 1 dataset (MIMIC). We cover 6 topologies with systematic taxonomy + ASVs + correction guidance. Their work is a stepping stone; ours is the full framework. [Experiments: plan_001]\n65\t\n66\t3. **\"Why these 6 DAG topologies?\"** → These are the canonical CSS causal designs used by Hernán & Cole (2009) and TeBlunthuis et al. (2024). They cover the major roles an annotated variable can play: confounder, mediator, collider, instrument, front-door criterion variable. [Experiments: plan_001]\n67\t\n68\t4. **\"K≥5 expressions aren't actionable\"** → Three interpretability tools: (a) eigendecomposition summaries identifying dominant bias modes, (b) worst-case scalar bounds, (c) diagnostic visualizations. [Experiments: plan_002]\n69\t\n70\t5. **\"ASV sharpness only proved for 3 topologies\"** → Honest scoping reflecting algebraic complexity. Remaining 3 validated via MC with numerical sharpness evidence. Full proofs identified as immediate future work. [Experiments: plan_004, plan_005]\n71\t\n72\t6. **\"Correlated multi-LLM errors?\"** → Kim et al. (ICML 2025) actually STRENGTHENS our case: if ensembles can't fix errors (60% agreement when both err), error structure analysis is the only viable approach. Multi-annotator correction with dependence is identified as an open problem. [Framework discussion]\n73\t\n74\t7. **\"Empirical validation scope\"** → 3 CSS tasks × 3 LLMs × 3 DAG-relevant scenarios. Tasks selected to cover K=2,3,5 annotation complexity. [Experiments: plan_003, plan_006]\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91a9ab6d-de69-4f3c-8c74-05f4d04786ff","uuid":"a23560cd-4773-43c0-aa90-7af65f0eb342","timestamp":"2026-05-20T08:24:24.290Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod_0510/projects/causal_annot_error_attrib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T074549/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — causal_annot_error_attrib\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract: 2026-07-21, paper: 2026-07-28)\n- Secondary: ARR October 2026 (~2026-10-15, commit to NAACL 2027 / ACL 2027)\n- Tertiary: ICWSM 2027 Round 2 (2026-09-15)\n\n## Title\nMulti-Class Annotation Errors Are Not Created Equal: Bias Taxonomy, Sensitivity Analysis, and Correction Guidance for LLM-Annotated Causal Studies\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n1. **Multi-class bias taxonomy**: For K≥3 annotation variables, the direction and magnitude of downstream bias depend on the interaction between the K×K confusion matrix structure and the DAG topology — breaking guarantees that hold in the binary case. We provide closed-form bias expressions for K=3 and matrix-form expressions with interpretability tools for general K, across 6 canonical social-science DAG topologies (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, instrument, front-door).\n\n2. **Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs)**: A sensitivity analysis framework for annotation-induced bias. ASVs quantify the minimum degree of differential annotation error needed to overturn a downstream conclusion, parameterized by a reference confusion matrix and amplification factor δ. Reported alongside estimates, analogous to E-values for unmeasured confounding. Formal proofs (monotonicity + sharpness) for 3 priority topologies; MC-validated conjectures for the remaining 3.\n\n3. **Correction guidance framework**: A principled decision framework mapping {DAG topology × error structure × ASV} to the appropriate correction method (DSL, PPI, MLA, naive plug-in), characterizing regimes where each applies and where no existing method suffices — including dependence-aware multi-LLM correction as an open problem.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: LLMs are rapidly replacing human annotators in CSS, but Baumann et al. (2025) show 31% of downstream conclusions are already incorrect. Researchers lack diagnostic tools to answer: (a) which annotation error structures propagate bias under their causal design, and (b) how robust their conclusions are to annotation error. Kim et al. (ICML 2025) show cross-model errors are highly correlated (60% agreement when both err), meaning ensembles cannot escape systematic bias — researchers MUST analyze the error structure of their chosen annotator, which is exactly what our tools enable.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Wood-Doughty et al. (2018) apply DAG reasoning to text classifier errors but produce no formal bias theorems (binary only). TeBlunthuis et al. (2024) provide the most complete binary treatment with MLA correction but are limited to K=2, single classifier. Lee & Wood-Doughty (2024) extend to categorical + LLM classifiers but cover only 1 DAG topology (unobserved confounding), with no systematic taxonomy or sensitivity analysis. VanderWeele & Li (2019) provide sensitivity bounds but only for binary risk ratios. Jerzak et al. (EMNLP 2025) benchmark correction methods but treat error as monolithic — if different confusion matrix structures produce qualitatively different bias in different DAG topologies, unconditional benchmarking is itself misleading.\n\n- **Our framing**: Multi-class annotation errors are not created equal. The same confusion matrix that produces innocuous residual bias in one DAG topology can produce direction-reversing toxic bias in another. We provide the first systematic characterization of this interaction (the taxonomy), a tool to quantify robustness (ASVs), and guidance for when/how to correct (the framework). This is not a new correction method — it is the missing diagnostic layer that tells researchers which correction is appropriate, or whether correction is needed at all.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"Across 6 DAG topologies with K=3,5,7, our bias expressions correctly predict bias direction and magnitude within 95% MC confidence intervals. On 3 CSS tasks annotated by 3 LLMs, ASVs provide calibrated robustness indicators, and our taxonomy identifies cases where commonly applied corrections would increase rather than decrease bias.\"\n\n## Core Insight (Lead Finding) — DUAL VERSION, gate on plan_001\n\n### Version A (≥1 topology shows direction reversal under K≥3 non-differential exposure misclassification)\nThe binary guarantee that \"non-differential exposure misclassification biases toward the null\" (Bross 1954) breaks in the multi-class case. For specific confusion matrix structures in specific DAG topologies, K≥3 non-differential error can amplify or reverse effect estimates. Brenner (1993) identified this possibility for polytomous confounder misclassification (\"bias can go in either direction\"); we provide the first systematic characterization across 6 DAG topologies with exact conditions for each {C structure × topology} cell. Furthermore, binarizing a K-class variable to \"escape\" into the binary regime is itself hazardous — collapsing introduces new, direction-unpredictable bias (MVP evidence: Δ up to 0.103).\n\n### Version B (no direction reversal found across topologies)\nMulti-class annotation error is structurally irreducible to the binary case: the downstream bias magnitude depends on the full K×K confusion matrix in ways that binary sensitivity/specificity cannot predict, and binarizing a K-class variable introduces new bias (MVP evidence: Δ up to 0.103). While non-differential error preserves bias direction across tested topologies (a useful practitioners' guarantee), the magnitude varies dramatically with confusion matrix structure (0.13–0.30 in confounding DAG alone), and this variation is topology-dependent. ASVs provide the missing robustness measure for this quantitative — not directional — uncertainty.\n\n**Gate**: plan_001 results determine which version. ≥2 topologies with reversal → A; 1 → A with that topology as lead; 0 → B. See D003.\n\n## MVP Evidence\n\n**Confounding DAG + K=3** (confounder misclassification):\n- Theoretical bias expressions match MC perfectly (6/6 structures within 95% CI) — framework validated\n- Binary collapse ≠ multi-class: Δ up to 0.103 (near-permutation C), proving K=3 retains more confounding control than binarization\n- 100k random C search: confounding DAG bias is monotonically toward OVB — this topology is \"well-behaved\" (predictable direction)\n- NOTE: Confounding DAG tests residual confounding, NOT bias-toward-null (Bross 1954). Criterion 2 (away-from-null bias) holds for binary too in this topology. See M001.\n\n**Exposure DAG K=3** (exposure misclassification — D002/D004 CONFIRMED):\n- 100k random C: 85% violate bias-toward-null, 83.4% sign reversal, 14.1% amplification\n- K=2 control: 6/6 toward null (binary guarantee holds)\n- Worst case: β=1.0 → estimate=-0.84 (sign reversal + 2.83x amplification)\n- Mechanism: near-permutation C causes label swap → effect direction flips (structurally impossible in K=2)\n- **Two-DAG contrast**: confounding (monotonic, predictable) vs exposure (unpredictable, topology-dependent) — validates core claim\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **AAAI**: Broad AI audience — emphasize general methodology for \"automated annotation measurement error,\" not LLM-specific. Frame as extending classical measurement error theory to modern automated labeling.\n- **ARR/NAACL**: NLP audience — CSS track is natural fit. May want more empirical NLP experiments. Balance theory with practical LLM annotation scenarios.\n- **Cross-discipline bridge**: Epidemiology (measurement error theory) × NLP (annotation errors). Epi reviewers check proof rigor; NLP reviewers check empirical relevance. Must serve both.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n\n1. **\"Trivial extension of binary to multi-class\"** → Brenner (1993) proved direction reversal; our taxonomy provides the first systematic characterization across 6 topologies. Binarizing K-class introduces NEW unpredictable bias (MVP evidence). The complexity jump is structural: binary has 2 free parameters (sensitivity, specificity) → scalar bias; K=5 has 20 free parameters → matrix-valued bias requiring eigendecomposition for interpretation. [Experiments: plan_001, plan_008]\n\n2. **\"Lee & Wood-Doughty (2024) already did categorical\"** → They cover 1 topology (unobserved confounding) on 1 dataset (MIMIC). We cover 6 topologies with systematic taxonomy + ASVs + correction guidance. Their work is a stepping stone; ours is the full framework. [Experiments: plan_001]\n\n3. **\"Why these 6 DAG topologies?\"** → These are the canonical CSS causal designs used by Hernán & Cole (2009) and TeBlunthuis et al. (2024). They cover the major roles an annotated variable can play: confounder, mediator, collider, instrument, front-door criterion variable. [Experiments: plan_001]\n\n4. **\"K≥5 expressions aren't actionable\"** → Three interpretability tools: (a) eigendecomposition summaries identifying dominant bias modes, (b) worst-case scalar bounds, (c) diagnostic visualizations. [Experiments: plan_002]\n\n5. **\"ASV sharpness only proved for 3 topologies\"** → Honest scoping reflecting algebraic complexity. Remaining 3 validated via MC with numerical sharpness evidence. Full proofs identified as immediate future work. [Experiments: plan_004, plan_005]\n\n6. **\"Correlated multi-LLM errors?\"** → Kim et al. (ICML 2025) actually STRENGTHENS our case: if ensembles can't fix errors (60% agreement when both err), error structure analysis is the only viable approach. Multi-annotator correction with dependence is identified as an open problem. [Framework discussion]\n\n7. **\"Empirical validation scope\"** → 3 CSS tasks × 3 LLMs × 3 DAG-relevant scenarios. Tasks selected to cover K=2,3,5 annotation complexity. [Experiments: plan_003, plan_006]\n","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":75}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LawLZTUDpaTG49K6f9B5cr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the data I need. Let me also check if the paper-writing-norms skill exists and read it for style guidance.\n\nActually, I need to read the paper-writing-norms skill for LaTeX writing best practices.\n\nLet me also check if pdflatex is available and if the output directory exists.\n\nLet me proceed to write the full LaTeX paper. I have:\n\n1. 9 experiments with detailed results and tables\n2. Paper framing with title, claims, story spine, venue info\n3. Narrative guidance about Version A being confirmed\n\nLet me now structure the paper:\n\n- Abstract\n- Introduction (story spine + core claims)\n\n\n- Related Work\n- Method (bias taxonomy + ASV framework + correction guidance)\n- Experiments (all 9 experiments mapped to sections/tables)\n- Conclusion\n\nThe experiments map to:\n1. Multi-topology bias derivation (K=3) → Tables 1-2\n2. General K bias expressions (K=5,7) → Tables 3-4\n3. Empirical confusion matrices → Tables 5-6\n4. Binarization collapse → Table 7\n5. ASV formal proofs (3 priority) → Tables 8-9\n6. ASV MC validation (remaining 3) → Table 10\n7. ASV calibration on empirical data → Tables 11-12\n8. Correction guidance framework → Tables 13-14\n9. Pathological C analysis → Table 15\n10. Annotation-sensitivity toolkit → mentioned in text\n\nNow I'm ready to write the paper, pulling all numbers directly from the experiment data and making sure every result is grounded in what I've actually computed. I need to verify the directory structure and then generate the full manuscript with proper rigor.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23577,"cache_read_input_tokens":23647,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23577},"output_tokens":8,"service_tie